tickets.body:
### Clear and concise description of the problem

As a developer using Vitest I want `coverage.thresholds.autoUpdate` to receive
the previous threshold as a second argument so that I can tell apart V8 variance
from a real regression.

The current callback signature is `(newValue: number) => number`. Without the
old value there is no way to write logic like "only accept a drop of max X%"

### Suggested solution

Pass the current (pre-update) threshold as a second argument to the `autoUpdate`
callback. The value is already in scope inside `updateThresholds` as `thresholds[key]` — it just isn't forwarded to the formatter. The change is a single line and fully backwards-compatible (existing single-arg callbacks continue to work).


```ts
// packages/vitest/src/node/coverage.ts — updateThresholds()
// before
const formattedValue = thresholdFormatter(actual)
// after
const formattedValue = thresholdFormatter(actual, threshold ?? 100)
```

This enables a drift-tolerance pattern:
```ts
// vitest.config.ts
coverage: {
  thresholds: {
    autoUpdate: (newValue, oldValue) => {
      if ((oldValue - newValue) > X) {
        throw new Error(`Coverage dropped ${(oldValue - newValue).toFixed(1)}% — intentional?`)
      }
      return Math.max(0, Math.floor(newValue) - X)
    }
  }
}
```

### Alternative

_No response_

### Additional context

In my experience V8's native coverage can produce slightly different numbers between runs, which is why a fixed buffer feels necessary. Is this something others run into too? We would love to hear what buffer values people are using in practice.

I looked at the source and the change appears small enough that we would be happy to open a PR if the maintainers agree with the direction. The old threshold is already in scope when `thresholdsToUpdate` is built — it just needs to be
carried through to the formatter call, and the `autoUpdate` type signature updated to `(newValue: number, oldValue: number) => number`. 
Is that the right approach?

